{{$NEXT}}

  - "run_if_trial_<phase>" runs its command only on a trial build or
    release, "run_no_trial_<phase>" only when it is not a trial,
    "run_if_release_<phase>" only during "dzil release" and
    "run_no_release_<phase>" only outside of it. They reach the
    [Run::<Phase>] of their phase as "run_if_trial", "run_no_trial",
    "run_if_release" and "run_no_release", next to "run_<phase>" in the
    same plugin. For a "no_cpan" dist, any of the release-phase keys brings
    the Releaser, so it gets no FakeRelease.

  - The SUPPORT section is written when the dist has an issue tracker or an
    IRC resource.
  - Repository metadata comes from GithubMeta for a GitHub remote, from
    GiteaMeta for a Gitea or Forgejo remote and from [Repository] for any
    other remote; each of them reads origin, or the first remote when there
    is no origin. A repository without a remote builds without one and
    without empty bugtracker or repository entries in META.json.

  - The remote detection and GiteaMeta run git directly, without a shell,
    so GitHub, Gitea and Forgejo remotes are also recognised on native
    Windows, and a remote name reaches git as it is.

  - The prerequisites name what the bundle loads: perl 5.22, the minimum of
    Dist::Zilla and Pod::Weaver, Moose and namespace::autoclean. PAR::Packer
    1.031 is recommended for the local pp check; Module::Runtime and
    Software::License are test requirements.

  - New option family for dists that ship standalone binaries: "binary = name"
    (repeatable) with the optional "binary_modules", "binary_require",
    "binary_platforms", "binary_pp_args", "binary_verify", "binary_smoke" and
    "binary_prepare" keys adds one [@Author::GETTY::Binary / name] subsection
    per binary, and its plugin writes them as "x_binaries" into META.json.
    "binary_default = 0" leaves the subsections out. A binary release is
    published as assets of the GitHub release, so "dzil" now dies at build
    when "binary" is set together with "no_github" or "no_github_release",
    or when the dist has no GitHub remote (which makes "no_github" default
    to on). A trailing "::*" in "binary_modules" packs every module below the
    namespace at any depth, not the module itself (pp gets "-M Foo::**"), which
    is the namespace "binary_require" checks for "Foo::*".
    Dists without "binary" keys are unchanged.

  - The new plugin Author::GETTY::GitHubRelease replaces GitHub::CreateRelease
    for every dist with a GitHub remote: it creates the release as a draft,
    attaches the tarball and only then publishes it, so a release is never
    visible without its tarball. A failed upload or publish fails "dzil release"
    loudly and leaves the draft in place for inspection. The release is
    marked as latest unless the dist ships binaries.

  - A "task" dist stamps Changes with [NextRelease] in the same format and
    UTC time as the other dists, ahead of @Git, so the stamped Changes is
    committed with the release and GitHub::CreateRelease finds its notes.
    Test::ChangesHasContent checks it again (not with "no_changes").

  - A dist with "binary" keys now packs each declared binary with pp during
    "dzil build" and runs its verify and smoke commands on the result, so a
    broken binary already fails the local build. When pp cannot be started,
    the build names the reason. "dzil release" requires
    .github/workflows/binaries.yml calling the shared dzil-binary.yml and
    dies without it. The dzil-test action sets DZIL_BINARY_SKIP=1, so CI
    builds skip the pp step. On native Windows the local pp step starts pp
    with the building perl, writes the binary as NAME.exe and hands
    binary_pp_args and the verify and smoke commands to sh unchanged. The
    verify commands run without PERL5LIB, so the binary under test does not
    see the libraries of its dependencies, and get the PERL5LIB value under
    which the dependencies load as DZIL_BINARY_PERL5LIB (in the CI leg the
    local lib of the dependencies, in the local check the PERL5LIB of the
    caller, empty when there is none); a verify command that needs a perl
    loading the dist's modules runs "PERL5LIB=$DZIL_BINARY_PERL5LIB perl
    -Ilib ...". The smoke commands run without either variable. Dists
    without "binary" keys are unchanged.

  - Build-leg helper scripts for the shared binary release workflow live in
    .github/dzil-binary (plan.pl, pp-args.pl, require-gate.pl, lib-gate.pl,
    win-dlls.pl).
    They plan the platform legs, assemble the pp command line, gate the
    packed binary on its declared requirements and fail it when it links a
    shared library other than the system ones or needs a glibc newer than
    2.28. lib-gate.pl also starts each packed binary once (--help, stdin
    at EOF, a fresh PAR_GLOBAL_TEMP, 60 s bound), checks every native
    object that first start extracts (libperl, the inner executable, PAR's
    own XS) like the packed ones, and fails when no perl library was
    extracted, and a tool it runs (ldd, objdump, otool) that is killed by
    a signal now fails the gate. On Windows the pack step also links (pp -l)
    the DLLs from the packing perl's own perl\bin and c\bin that the packed
    XS needs and the gate would otherwise reject as foreign, such as
    Strawberry's OpenSSL. No effect on dists without "binary" keys.

  - The shared binary release workflow gets its publish-job helper
    .github/dzil-binary/publish.pl (render, expected, sums, check) and the
    install script templates install.sh.in and install.ps1.in. The rendered
    install scripts download the binaries of a GitHub release, verify them
    against SHA256SUMS and install them; install.ps1 defaults to
    %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\<dist name>. No effect on dists without "binary"
    keys.

  - The shared binary release workflow .github/workflows/dzil-binary.yml
    (workflow_call, inputs "tag" and "bundle_ref") builds the binaries a
    dist declares on every platform leg with a packing perl of its own
    (.github/dzil-binary/build-perl.sh) and the leg phases of
    .github/dzil-binary/build-leg.sh, gates and verifies them, smoke-tests
    them on clean images and runners, and attaches them with SHA256SUMS,
    install.sh and install.ps1 to the GitHub release. An empty "tag" is a
    probe run that builds the checked-out commit and touches no release;
    its version slot is "probe-<sha7>". A dist calls it from
    .github/workflows/binaries.yml at @binary-v1. No effect on dists
    without "binary" keys. Its publish job marks a release as latest only
    when there is no latest release yet or the latest one is not newer, so
    re-running an older release never rolls releases/latest back, and it
    fails on an empty list of expected binaries. .gitattributes keeps
    .github/dzil-binary at LF line endings on every checkout. Archive::Zip
    is a test requirement.

0.327     2026-10-26 00:40:01Z

  - The CI documentation says when the "v1" tag of the dzil-test action
    moves, so it is clear that a dist pinned to "@main" gets every change at
    once while "@v1" gets it when the maintainer moves the tag.

  - A "no_cpan" dist gets [FakeRelease] in place of the removed
    UploadToCPAN, so "dzil release" no longer aborts with "can't release
    without any Releaser plugins" and still tags, bumps the version and
    pushes. It is left out when the dist has a Releaser of its own (the
    default Docker subsection or run_release).

  - Test::ChangesHasContent is no longer added with "no_changes" or "task":
    there is no version heading in Changes then, so its release test could
    only fail.

  - The documentation matches the code: the "equivalent to" listing shows
    the plugins configure() really assembles, and the Docker subsection no
    longer documents a "context" option that was never passed on. The
    cpanfile requires Dist::Zilla::Plugin::Authority 1.009, the version the
    bundle demands at build time.

  - The documented POD commands =resource and =event now work (they were
    passed through as unknown commands and broke POD on MetaCPAN), and the
    docs say that =seealso, =synopsis and =description take no argument.

  - The SUPPORT section no longer says bugs are reported "on GitHub": the
    tracker URL may be a Codeberg or Forgejo one, so the sentence just names
    the link.

  - GitHub and Gitea/Forgejo detection asks git for the remotes instead of
    parsing .git/config, so a dist checked out as a linked worktree or a
    submodule keeps GithubMeta/GiteaMeta, the bugtracker metadata and
    GitHub::CreateRelease. The host is taken from origin, as GiteaMeta does,
    not from whichever remote is listed first.

  - The link to https://raudssus.de/ is added to the COPYRIGHT AND LICENSE
    section by the weaver bundle, so copyright_holder can stay plain text and
    the generated README and LICENSE no longer print "L<...>" markup. A holder
    that still carries the link is not given it twice.

  - The option conflicts (weaver_config with task, author with no_cpan,
    no_install with no_makemaker) now abort with their own message instead
    of "Can't locate object method log_fatal".

  - The Docker subsection no longer sets build_load or passes release_load
    through: Docker::API has dropped both, as nothing ever read them, and
    would warn on every build. The platform option documents that it takes
    exactly one value.

  - New "no_license" attribute: the bundle neither generates a LICENSE nor
    demands a committed one. "no_cpan" now implies it, so a private dist no
    longer aborts with "no LICENSE in the distribution". A LICENSE that is
    committed anyway is still shipped, just no longer checked against
    dist.ini. Set "no_license = 0" to keep the check, or
    "generate_license = 1" to keep the generated file.

  - The shared dzil-test CI action installs from https://cpan.metacpan.org
    by default (same default as the SimpiCI provider). The runner needs an
    https-capable cpanm (curl, wget or IO::Socket::SSL, plus CA
    certificates); the official perl images have that. "cpanm-opts" still
    overrides the default.

  - The SimpiCI provider runs its dzil test jobs in prebuilt images,
    ghcr.io/getty/dzil-perl:<ver> (perl:<ver> with Dist::Zilla, this bundle
    and its author-test deps preinstalled), rebuilt weekly for every version
    in perl-versions, so a job no longer installs ~200 CPAN modules first.
    The job files are now named "ghcr.io+getty+dzil-perl+<ver>+test.sh": a
    dist overriding one version's job ships that name, while a
    "perl+<ver>+test.sh" of its own now runs in addition.

0.326     2026-09-29 17:20:32Z

  - Every distribution built with [@Author::GETTY] now snapshots its agent
    context (.claude/.codex, CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, skilletor.lock.json, ...)
    into the build under misc/agent-context/ (present in the tarball, not
    installed), via Dist::Zilla::Plugin::GatherAgentContext. This records which
    skills, agents and rules a release was built with, including
    skilletor-installed (git-ignored) ones. New prereq:
    Dist::Zilla::Plugin::GatherAgentContext.

  - Git::NextVersion version_regexp is now derived from tag_format, so a dist
    without $VERSION in its source finds its last release tag instead of
    restarting at first_version.

0.325     2026-09-22 06:46:49Z

  - Version rewriting defaults to ":InstallModules" and ":PerlExecFiles", so
    modules and Perl executables receive the release version while non-Perl
    programs under "bin/" remain installed without being parsed as Perl.

0.324     2026-09-19 01:05:02Z

  - F<.karr> is now excluded from the gathered distribution files for CPAN
    releases, so it never lands in a consuming dist's release tarball. No
    new toggle -- reuses the existing gather_exclude_filename mechanism
    alongside the README.md exclusion, and is skipped when C<no_cpan> is
